{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n15 \n \n \n \nFigure 4: Crew-exchangeable radiometer arrays on the ACME chamber insert: (top) standard \n(ACME) array of 5 radiometers, and (bottom) array with three radiometers, designed for the BRE \nexperiment, where the lowest detector is masked to avoid view of the burner. \n \nA third radiometer array was used in the CFI-G experiment and the second round of the Flame \nDesign experiment, where it is important to understand that only a single radiometer array can be \nused at a time.  Unlike ACME\u2019s other radiometer arrays, each sensor in the CFI-G array has two \nfixed gains, i.e., a high gain and low gain.  It is described in the Cool Flames Radiometer \nCharacterization Report (CIR-RPT-4600). \n \nThe narrow-view radiometers in the standard array are only fully sensitive in small spots, but they \nare each sensitive in a much greater Field of View (FOV) than represented in the preceding figure.   \nThey do not have lenses; their FOV is a result of an aperture where most of their FOV is within the \npenumbra which is only visible to a portion of the sensor.  Figures representing the measured FOVs", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 15}}